- \(x\) is forced to block at \((2,5)\);
- \(o\) can then continue by placing at \((1,5)\), creating a direct threat at \((0,5)\);
- \(x\) is forced to block at \((0,5)\).

At the end of this forced exchange both of \(o\)'s original winning squares and \(x\)'s only original winning square have been permanently occupied. The side that possesses the next free tempo therefore begins the secondary battle with a decisive initiative advantage.

Among the five non-losing first moves, only column 1 opens a viable secondary win:
- placing at \((1,1)\) produces the three-in-a-row \((1,1)\)-\((2,2)\)-\((3,3)\), making \((0,0)\) a new unconditional winning square for \(x\).
- Combined with any later three consecutive \(x\) stones in column 0 at rows 4-2, this yields an unstoppable pair of threats inside the same column: the vertical win at \((1,0)\) cannot be blocked without immediately enabling the diagonal win at \((0,0)\).

No other first-move column creates a comparable (or faster) realizable threat after the forced right-side exchange. Consequently the unique optimal opening is column 1, which claims the necessary free tempo on the only line that later produces a forced win.

